# Cross-Platform First (Android / iOS / Web)

This Flutter app ships to **Android, iOS, and web**. Every change must be valid and usable on all three unless the user explicitly scopes to one platform.

## Defaults

- Prefer shared Dart/Flutter APIs (`flutter/`, `package:` libs that support all targets).
- Do **not** import `dart:io` or `dart:html` from shared UI/feature code.
- Do **not** assume mobile-only behavior (background alarms, notifications, deep links, file paths, clipboard, sensors) works the same on web—or that web APIs exist on mobile.
- UI must work for touch **and** pointer, and for phone **and** desktop/web layouts (safe areas, overflow, keyboard, scroll).

## When platforms must differ

Use the existing split pattern—never scatter raw platform branches through features:

1. Shared interface / facade (e.g. `alarm_scheduler.dart`)
2. Conditional export/factory (`*_factory.dart` or `export '..._stub.dart' if (dart.library.html) '..._web.dart'`)
3. Implementations: `*_io.dart` (Android/iOS), `*_web.dart`, `*_stub.dart`

```dart
// ❌ BAD — breaks web compile or leaves a platform unimplemented
import 'dart:io';
if (Platform.isAndroid) { /* only Android */ }

// ✅ GOOD — shared call site, platform files behind conditional import
final scheduler = createAlarmScheduler();
await scheduler.schedule(alarm);
```

Use `kIsWeb` / `defaultTargetPlatform` only for small, unavoidable UI or config tweaks—not as a substitute for a proper platform implementation.

## Checklist before finishing

- [ ] Compiles for mobile **and** web (no `dart:io` / `dart:html` in shared paths)
- [ ] Feature has a real path on Android, iOS, **and** web (or an explicit stub with clear UX)
- [ ] No silent no-op on one platform unless the product intentionally disables it there
- [ ] Config/URLs/storage/auth respect platform differences already used in `app_config.dart` and web OAuth helpers
